  "deletedBy": null,
  "details": "1. Load into VRChat with someone else. \n\n2. Have them turn around without looking at a mirror or camera. \n\n3. Use an avatar that uses animation chains with small looping particles etc. \n\n4. While they have their back turned to you, try playing a sound or firing particles. \n\n5. Do they hear it? \n\nOptional, if you have looping audio or looping particles. \n\n1. Turn on the particles or looping audio while they are looking at you. \n\n2. Turn off the toggle for the particles or audio.\n\n3. They can still hear the audio or have the particles rendering on their local VRChat.\n\nIt appears when moving to 2022, a culling option may have accidentally been changed, this issue, in many instances, could lead to lots of looping particles from certain avatars or annoying looping sounds if you're not looking at the avatar causing it (by that I mean the bounding box of the avatar. I'm pretty sure at least.), while this doesn't work through walls (meaning, that if you stare at someone through a wall, their states still update properly), this can cause performance drops.\n\nThrough lots of testing with a good friend of mine, I've learnt that this is because when you turn away, their avatar on your instance remains on the last state machines that were active and when you look back, jumps straight to the current state without going through the states between point A and B. If Write Defaults is off and some certain things aren't specified in the current state to be turned off since the last time they looked, those things will still be toggled on/off from the last time you saw their avatar. this was not an issue with 2019 build as since there was a method to even properly sync animation chains across platforms. This has also lead to many avatars appearing broken, and since this culling doesn't take walls into account so it won't help performance much since you look in the direction of an avatar and they load in and update.\n\nSigns of this bug has existed since the migration but they've been soo small that they never raised any alarms in my head until now when I was helping a friend troubleshoot her avatar problems. \n\nThis problem affects animation chains in general and any animated property. The picture attached is a screenshot of an avatar exhibiting the animation bugs from the culling as mentioned.\n\nFirst how the avatar looked to me when I turned back around, vs how the avatar was on her screen, picture of me with my back turned in the picture to show that I followed my own steps.\n\nI'm glad Emitris helped me with getting these screenshots for the Bug report.\n\nCould not show the screenshots I had helped my friend with with her avatar since that was when we thought it was an Avatar issue and not a problem with the current version of VRChat.",
